title The harness the census could not see -- one resolver, two readers, and a label earned rather than softened
prompt Autonomous lap. Baton, card whole, rota row 3 (Water), day shelf, then the next agent-doable work in the language lane under reds-first.
think Round open: scoped cold roster pass, 105 guards run, 103 green, 0 red, 2 gated, 606s, run_verdict=ok. Then the card whole, the rota, the fleet thread.
think Lap N = 4038, so 4038 mod 5 = 3 -- the WATER row, care and flow. The previous lap read fire, so no repeat and no hand-advance. Water's sense is taste, which only works up close: read the actual file, run the actual witness. That is exactly how this lap went, and the cardinal seat set its shape -- Gall asks what smaller thing already works and whether this can grow from it.
think Reds-first picked the lap rather than claim-as-override. REDS %466 stood OPEN and its own last clause read *Not taken: the label and one header sentence are pheromone's, priced here rather than edited in their lane*. An OPEN row in my lane, reserved for it by name.
think I re-derived the peer's numbers before building on them. never=178, of which 114 are rye/tests -- 64%, reproducing the row's figure exactly. The harness names 116 stems, all 116 stand on disk, 114 of them in never and 2 credited by a literal elsewhere. 114 + 2 = 116, the identity the row proved, reproduced here.
think THE DEPTH QUESTION WAS THE WHOLE LAP. The row prices the repair as prose: rename the label, soften the header. That is honest and it leaves the instrument blind -- a reader would get a truthful LABEL over a number that still counts 114 compiled files as uncompiled. The crux is the other direction: teach the census to see, and the label becomes earned rather than merely accurate about a narrower question.
think And Gall answered how. The resolver already existed one file over: rye_harness_roster_scan.sh reads a harness's directory and stem list for its own gate. Growing the census from that working simple thing beat teaching it to parse `let dir =` a second time -- a rule written twice is a rule two files may come to disagree about.
think ONE HAZARD I CAUGHT BEFORE WRITING IT. The roster scan hunts a literal assembled shape and its unresolved count stood at 10 against a ceiling of 10 -- zero slack. Spelling that shape into the census's own header, or into a control that plants a harness, would have red the ceiling on the lap that proved the flag. So the header describes the shape in words and stays out of the corpus on its own merit; only the control, which must spell it to plant it, joins the read-past list, by exact name and with the say-why.
obs THE MECHANISM, PLAINLY. rye_harness_roster_scan.sh takes a new --paths argument: it short-circuits before the build-site awk, then prints one `harness_path <script> <dir>/<stem>.rye` line per stem, followed by `paths_mode=1`, `harnesses=`, `harness_units=`, `unresolved=` and a verdict. rye_compile_reach_scan.sh runs it, requires the marker, and folds every harness_path whose script stands in compiling.txt into named_compiling.txt before resolve_names, so those paths become build roots and the @import graph closes over them. Two new counters ride out: harness_paths and harness_unresolved. Argument parsing in the roster scan moved from a bare test to a case that refuses an unknown flag.
obs THE CREDIT IS CONDITIONAL, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SEAM. One scan answers what a harness assembles; the other answers whether that harness compiles. Neither knows the other's question. It is the same cut my last lap made between route_hops and point_hops, and it arrived from the opposite direction: there I split one function that answered two questions, here I joined two scans that each answer one.
obs THE PEN TAUGHT ME SOMETHING THE PLAN HAD WRONG. I wrote the refusal phase expecting asserted=3 -- a harness that only greps its programs, so its stems stand accused. It read asserted=0. The gated reading is the never set intersected with paths a READING runner spells LITERALLY, and a harness spells none of them; that is what made them invisible to begin with. So a program only a harness ever names can never enter the gate, built or unbuilt. That bound is asserted in the pen and written into the header now, and files_unlisted one file over is the guard that does reach it. My expectation was wrong, not the scan.
obs A MISSING INSTRUMENT REFUSES RATHER THAN ACCUSES. Without the resolver every assembled path reads never-compiled, which on this field is a confident false claim about 114 files -- so an absent resolver reads roster_scan_absent and one too old to know the flag reads roster_scan_unusable. The marker rides ahead even of empty_corpus, because a tree holding no harness and a copy that cannot answer are indistinguishable in the paths alone and only one of them is a fault. All four cases are planted, and so is the welcome an over-tightening would swallow: a harnessless tree exits 0.
obs FOUR MISCITATIONS REPAIRED ON TOUCH. The scan and its witness read %453 -- *a gate written as a floor* -- where %449 booked this census, and the same file already calls that fault %449's two paragraphs later. Verified against both shelved rows rather than from memory.
obs A COUNT I REMOVED RATHER THAN RESET. The roster witness header claimed *Twenty behaviors* and its closing line *eight refusals bitten, twelve honest readings left free*. My seven phases falsified all three -- the card's own lantern, a number a hand typed that a landing falsifies. Rather than write 27/15/12 for the next lap to falsify, the header now says which behaviors and lets the control's own cases= carry the total. Count, never number.
obs AND THE EXCLUSION MADE ONE READING TRUER. Dropping rye_compile_reach_control from the roster corpus took sites_unparsed 16 -> 9 and build_sites 2122 -> 2115: seven of the sixteen prose residues were that control's own printfs. The residue got smaller and truer rather than lost.
obs %440 FIRED AN EIGHTH AND A NINTH TIME IN THIS ONE SEND, AND MY OWN MISTAKE COST A PASS. The rebase auto-merged the day shelf and left 20260906.090503 standing above 091519 -- one pair, repaired by the same sort the last three firings took by hand. The writer for it is booked to BAKERY beside the nib writer that already landed, so I repaired and named it rather than building a second one in another seat's lane. Separately and squarely mine: I ran that repair WHILE the post-rebase roster pass was running, which the card forbids in one line -- hold still while it runs -- so that pass reads tree_moved and a clean one had to follow it. The rule is not decoration; the runner digests the tree at open and close precisely so a green cannot describe a tree that no longer exists. Then the SECOND rebase did it again -- one stamp duplicated and three pairs out of order, including a pair from six hours earlier that neither rebase had touched, which is the tell that the shelf accumulates disorder rather than suffering it once. Two hand repairs in one send, the same dedupe-and-sort both times, and the fifth and sixth I have watched a peer or myself perform today.
